<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Human sacrifice</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as sacrificial</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Human sacrifice is the ritual killing of people as an offering to gods or spirits. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a> it was central to religion, intended to sustain the gods and the cosmic order. The need for captives shaped Aztec warfare, including the ritualised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flower-wars">Flower Wars</a>, and was tied to tribute and imperial power.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Fomena that followed required Asante to pay an indemnity of 50,000 ounces of gold, renounce claims to the southern states and Elmina, and end <b>human sacrifice</b>, and it encouraged several former tributaries to break away, fracturing the empire's provincial network and triggering civil strife.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>At the same time the statesman Tlacaelel reshaped Mexica history to supply the theological foundation for empire: the Mexica were recast as the chosen people of the sun and war god Huitzilopochtli, whose continued existence required the nourishment of <b>human sacrifice</b>, making perpetual war a cosmic necessity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Royal power was renewed in the Annual Customs, ceremonies honouring the royal ancestors at which the king displayed his wealth, received tribute, distributed gifts and offered <b>human sacrifices</b>, whose scale European visitors often exaggerated but which were real.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-dahomey">Kingdom of Dahomey · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Religion was bound up with <b>human sacrifice</b>, and fanged gods appear throughout the art, among them the being known today as the Decapitator, who often holds a knife and a severed head.</blockquote><a href="/empire/moche">Moche · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Human sacrifice</b> was practised on a large scale: oracle-bone texts record the killing of thousands of captives, many of them Qiang, and royal tombs contain hundreds of victims.</blockquote><a href="/empire/shang-dynasty">Shang dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…central plain long regarded Qin as semi-barbarian, close to the Rong in its customs, and its elite practised <b>human sacrifice</b>: the Shiji records that 177 people followed Duke Mu of Qin into death in 621 BCE, and the excavated tomb at Yong attributed to Duke Jing of Qin…</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-qin">State of Qin · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
